# The WebOS rice

Everything here is installed to `/usr/share/webos/rice/` and applied to a home
directory by `webos-rice`. The package that owns this directory is
`webos-theme/webos-theme`. This is the only WebOS appearance: the mono rice.
Advanced dotfiles profiles remain available as expert CLI options, but the
installer does not expose an appearance page.

## The look

One idea, applied everywhere: **pure black, near-opaque, blurred, with soft-white
highlights and no coloured accents.** Panels are black at 92% opacity and let
the compositor blur the wallpaper through them.

| Token | Value | Used by |
|---|---|---|
| base | `#000000` | terminals, wallpaper |
| surface | `rgba(0,0,0,0.92)` | waybar, wofi, mako, wlogout, music/stats dropdowns |
| text | `#D8D8D8` | primary labels, terminal foreground |
| accent | `#E8E8E8` | focus, active workspace, icons, cursor, borders |
| dim | `#7A7A7A` | secondary labels |
| faint | `#4A4A4A` | empty workspace buttons |
| border | `rgba(232,232,232,0.20)` | pill and panel outlines |
| border-hover | `rgba(232,232,232,0.35)` | hover/focus outlines |

The greyscale ramp is `#a6a6a6` → `#c2c2c2` → `#d8d8d8` → `#e8e8e8`; where a
plain RGB triplet is required (fastfetch, Konsole, the Plasma colour scheme),
use `216,216,216` for text and `122,122,122` for dim/secondary.

The palette is deliberately repeated across several files rather than generated
from a template — a template that fails silently is worse than an explicit list.
Change it in all of these places:

* `hypr/webos-colors.lua`, `hypr/hyprland.lua`, `hypr/hyprlock.conf`
* `waybar/config.jsonc`, `waybar/style.css`
* `wofi/style.css`, `wlogout/style.css`
* `kitty/kitty.conf`, `foot/foot.ini`, `fish/functions/fish_prompt.fish`
* `fastfetch/fastfetch.json`, `mako/config`
* `gtk-3.0/gtk.css`, `gtk-4.0/gtk.css`
* `kvantum/kvantum.kvconfig`
* `konsole/DeepBlack.colorscheme`, `konsole/DeepBlack.profile`,
  `color-schemes/DeepBlack.colors`, `plasma/kdeglobals`
* the scripts beside the rice in `/usr/share/webos/`: `webos-wallpaper`,
  `webos-fastfetch` and `webos-chafa-logo` (ANSI colour codes and accent cycling)

## Where transparency comes from

Hyprland does the blur, not the individual apps:

```lua
hl.config({
    decoration = { blur = { enabled = true, size = 7, passes = 3 } },
})
hl.layer_rule({ match = { namespace = "^waybar$" }, blur = true })
hl.layer_rule({ match = { namespace = "^wofi$" },   blur = true })
```

Applications only need to *be* translucent — `background_opacity 0.82` in
kitty, `alpha=0.86` in foot, an alpha channel in the waybar CSS. If a window is
opaque, blur does nothing to it, which is exactly what we want for Steam and
games.

Games are explicitly excluded from every effect:

```lua
hl.window_rule({ match = { class = "^(steam)$" },     opacity = "1.0 override 1.0 override" })
hl.window_rule({ match = { class = "^(steam|gamescope|wine)$" }, no_blur = true })
hl.window_rule({ match = { class = "^(gamescope)$" }, rounding = 0 })
```

## Applying it

```bash
webos-rice --user     # apply the rice to $HOME
webos-rice --system   # apply to /etc/skel and every user in /home (root)
webos-rice --check    # show what would change, change nothing
webos-rice --force    # overwrite files you have modified
emerge --config webos-theme/webos-theme   # same as --system --force
```

`webos-rice` never overwrites a file it did not write. Every file it manages
carries a marker line; if the marker is missing it assumes you took the file
over and leaves it alone (unless `--force`).

## Adding a machine-local tweak

Do not edit `/usr/share/webos/rice` — it is replaced on upgrade. Instead:

* Hyprland: add `~/.config/hypr/local.lua`. `hyprland.lua` loads it with
  `require("local")` when it exists, so you can call `hl.config`, `hl.bind`
  and the rest without editing the managed file.
* Waybar: put a second bar in `~/.config/waybar/config.jsonc` under a different
  `name`, or override a module in `~/.config/waybar/style.css`.
* Everything else: the file is yours once you edit it.

## Wallpaper

`webos-wallpaper` renders `wallpapers/webos-wallpaper.svg` to PNG at install
time (it needs `rsvg-convert` or ImageMagick), writes it to
`/usr/share/webos/wallpaper/`, and tells hyprpaper to reload. It also writes a
darker variant used by the lock screen.

```bash
webos-wallpaper              # regenerate from the shipped SVG
webos-wallpaper --dark       # regenerate the dark variant only
webos-wallpaper --next       # cycle the greyscale accent (regenerates the PNG)
```

The shipped `webos-wallpaper.svg` is pure black, so `--dark` produces the same
image; both commands stay for compatibility with the waybar module.
